    "ordinal": 40,
    "locator": "::exercise-40",
    "anchor": null,
    "line": 1502,
    "label": "Exercise C.3 \u2014 A Gaussian posterior and a safety probability",
    "level": null,
    "source_sha256": "bf2b63f0065f7701027e820af7fe0eb6da905ae61e414e75741ba49867226a87",
    "source_text": "\n      \n        Exercise C.3 \u2014 A Gaussian posterior and a safety probability\n      \n      \n        Prior $f\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)$. Four measurements $y_i=f+\\varepsilon_i$ with $\\varepsilon_i\\sim\\mathcal N(0,0.25)$ i.i.d. and independent of $f$ have mean $\\bar y=0.9$. (a) Compute the posterior mean and variance with precision addition. (b) Compute $\\mathbb P(f\\lt0.5\\mid\\text{data})$. (c) Is $f\\ge0.3$ certified by the lower bound $\\mu-2\\sigma$, and what is the posterior probability that this bound fails?\n        \n          Show answer\n          \n            (a) $\\bar y$ has noise variance $0.25/4=0.0625$, i.e. precision $16$. Posterior precision $1+16=17$, variance $1/17=0.0588$ (standard deviation $0.2425$), mean $(1\\cdot0+16\\cdot0.9)/17=0.847$.\n            (b) $\\Phi\\big((0.5-0.847)/0.2425\\big)=\\Phi(-1.431)=0.076$.\n            (c) $\\mu-2\\sigma=0.847-0.485=0.362\\ge0.3$, so yes. The bound fails with posterior probability $\\Phi(-2)=0.023$. This is a Bayesian statement inside the model; a whole run of such decisions needs a union bound over them (Section 6).\n          \n        \n      \n    ",
    "text_sha256": "24f734e641065079875c0f07b7c56e5150c3dd907827c1a5de130198a1a07002"
  },
